#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096423 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "75fd35db-6b4e-5820-b6ac-925d24a0f117" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Left or Right-Opening Parabola with Vertex at the Origin Given a Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "7f3646c9-89b6-58de-a363-cb87e034d9f0" :question/math-academy-id "e-5333" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041337/assets/70ff1c84aa4a9a2adc393fc1dcf684ee0bcf161671b15f9061e2319f5d7e0a39.png)\n\n  \n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that the arms of the parabola open to the left. The parabola has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(-8,4).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\ny^2  = 4px.\n$$\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(-8,4),$ we can substitute $x = -8$ and $y = 4$ into the standard equation and solve for $p\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {4}^{2} & =4p(-8) \\\\ 16 & =-32p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(-\\frac{1}{2})x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the left since $p = -\\dfrac{1}{2}$ is negative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096424 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17099881-d7b9-54d9-a29a-4f5f28561cd3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Left or Right-Opening Parabola with Vertex at the Origin Given Another Point"}] :question/id #uuid "d66f0111-fe31-5381-ab6d-cd67ee403d28" :question/math-academy-id "e-5334" :question/problem "Find the equation of the right-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(4,4).$" :question/worked-solution "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\ny^2  = 4px.\n$$\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(4,4),$ we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=4$ into the standard equation and solve for $p\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {4}^{2} & =4p(4) \\\\ 16 & =16p \\\\ p & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(1)x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096425 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc86c375-c2c4-56b7-a207-0abfc9709f6a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Left or Right-Opening Parabola Given a Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "5546750b-01ad-5aaa-93de-b223297ffc0a" :question/math-academy-id "e-5335" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041337/assets/55651f756b31405e08cd96b097101528ffabff1e6411d3fcae9f40f747f607b4.png)\n\n  \n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the left. The parabola has its vertex at $(4,2)$ and passes through the point $(0,6).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h, k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h, k) = (4,2),$ we substitute $h=4$ and $k=2$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =4p(x-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(0,6),$ so we can substitute $x=0$ and $y=6$ into the equation above and solve for $p\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =4p(x-4) \\\\ (6-2)^{2} & =4p(0-4) \\\\ 16 & =-16p \\\\ p & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =4p(x-4) \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =4(-1)(x-4) \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =-4(x-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the left since $p = -1$ is negative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096426 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "783ca59b-0242-584c-ae47-004a0eb03817" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Left or Right-Opening Parabola Given the Vertex and Another Point"}] :question/id #uuid "9a27e39b-4a17-578f-830b-3e143c65fd55" :question/math-academy-id "e-5336" :question/problem "Find the equation of the left-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(2,-2)$ and passes through the point $(-6,2).$" :question/worked-solution "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h, k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h, k) = (2,-2),$ we substitute $h=2$ and $k=-2$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+2)^{2} & =4p(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(-6, 2),$ so we can substitute $x=-6$ and $y=2$ into the equation above and solve for $p\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+2)^{2} & =4p(x-2) \\\\ (2+2)^{2} & =4p(-6-2) \\\\ 16 & =-32p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+2)^{2} & =4p(x-2) \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =4(-\\frac{1}{2})(x-2) \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =-2(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311471 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "75fd35db-6b4e-5820-b6ac-925d24a0f117" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Left or Right-Opening Parabola with Vertex at the Origin Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05285e91-6563-5d86-9ea1-5d8ab0c6b499" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=\\frac{4}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311473} {:answer/id #uuid "e6064e2a-e921-5d2e-8256-3fcb18b99120" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-\\frac{9}{2}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311474} {:answer/id #uuid "abaa488b-002f-5654-98fc-b9fd91f0605d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-\\frac{1}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311475} {:answer/id #uuid "0492805e-b19e-5b17-b646-be1a70c747f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=\\frac{3}{4}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311476} {:answer/id #uuid "abc04700-174b-5d32-a7a4-d01666565b6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=\\frac{9}{8}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311477}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05285e91-6563-5d86-9ea1-5d8ab0c6b499" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=\\frac{4}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311473} :answer-field/id #uuid "75cb4592-93f7-5a63-8588-acebaddca7b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311472}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d2494c4e-4dc3-5d72-87cc-1d73c73f8edf" :question/math-academy-id "q-115601" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041337/assets/4938d034c766b78e1da1a5bd19f2a12b314f772ca4819cd52d4f1463ff1ae3a4.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the right. The parabola has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(3,2).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(3,2),$ we can substitute $x=3$ and $y=2$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {2}^{2} & =4p(3) \\\\ 4 & =12p \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(\\frac{1}{3})x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{4}{3}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the right since $p=\\frac{1}{3}$ is positive."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311478 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "75fd35db-6b4e-5820-b6ac-925d24a0f117" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Left or Right-Opening Parabola with Vertex at the Origin Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa402c91-1fa2-5915-b3ee-0acd9fab9b9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=9x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311480} {:answer/id #uuid "863278e1-1baf-5375-9be9-5ba04c6ae397" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=\\frac{1}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311481} {:answer/id #uuid "3a5b98eb-0316-5683-9226-32a216b58c8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=\\frac{9}{4}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311482} {:answer/id #uuid "f34fd5e5-5c04-5e97-becb-4cfa44b4dfad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-9x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311483} {:answer/id #uuid "4d4123f9-9eee-5ae3-9336-37c9672c6daf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-\\frac{1}{12}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311484}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa402c91-1fa2-5915-b3ee-0acd9fab9b9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=9x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311480} :answer-field/id #uuid "a80d8690-cdb4-5371-964c-f34391dd22e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311479}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "63dd6ea0-6088-5bea-b162-dd607da9df2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-115602" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14041337/assets/5746a438eccdd55d345f1c71a768f971aa1a342798743e151f7557102ddd8783.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the right. The parabola has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(1,-3).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(1,-3),$ we can substitute $x=1$ and $y=-3$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ (-3)^{2} & =4p(1) \\\\ 9 & =4p \\\\ p & =\\frac{9}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(\\frac{9}{4})x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =9x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the right since $p=\\frac{9}{4}$ is positive."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311485 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "75fd35db-6b4e-5820-b6ac-925d24a0f117" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Left or Right-Opening Parabola with Vertex at the Origin Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5711b977-9b22-548d-9138-4275415392b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311487} {:answer/id #uuid "648142c1-d79f-56b8-b114-b2f58a05afbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311488} {:answer/id #uuid "be1bd756-c4a5-5aa7-b698-0c8d6f814ed6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311489} {:answer/id #uuid "9b56cd30-d59a-5ebe-a8d9-0d2e47503704" :answer/ty